  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Assessing Recharts Compatibility**\n\nI\'ve confirmed Recharts is installed, excellent! After checking, I see I already have other essential packages like Lucide React and React itself ready to go. I was wondering about its suitability for React 19, so I\'ll explore if it is compatible with React 19.\n\n\n**Organizing Our Files**\n\nI\'ve determined Recharts\' compatibility looks promising; any React 19 warnings should be manageable. My current focus is structuring the project. I\'m planning a modular approach, aiming for a clean and well-typed codebase. I\'ll define types in `/src/types.ts` and populate initial data in `/src/data.ts` to create an instantly functional application with realistic ERP data.\n\n\n**Defining Project Structure**\n\nI\'m now detailing the planned component structure. The `/src` directory will hold distinct modules like `/db_schema_sql.ts` (Supabase SQL script). I\'ll create components such as `Sidebar.tsx`, `Navbar.tsx`, `AuthPage.tsx`, and `Dashboard.tsx`. I\'ll also implement dedicated components for filament inventory (`Filaments.tsx`), purchase records (`Purchases.tsx`), 3D printer management (`Printers.tsx`), energy tariff tracking (`EnergyTariff.tsx`), finished product database (`Products.tsx`), production workflow (`Production.tsx`), and quote creation (`Budgets.tsx`).\n\n\n**Expanding Sales Functionality**\n\nI\'m now integrating sales modules. I\'m focusing on features such as sales order management, payment processing, delivery tracking, and automated stock adjustments within `/src/components/Sales.tsx`. Also, `/src/components/Clients.tsx` will house a detailed client directory. The `/src/components/Backup.tsx` component is designated for system settings, including backup/restore features and logs. I will also develop the `/src/App.tsx` file for the main shell, state coordination, authorization checks, and display. I\'m prioritizing safety by embracing modularity to avoid file size limitations, and the theme will be orange, black, and white. I\'ll write `/src/types.ts` next to define data types such as `User`, `Filament`, and `Purchase`, for the core ERP data.\n\n\n**Defining Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now detailing data structures for the ERP. I\'ve defined the data models for \'Printer\', \'EnergyTariff\', and \'BOMItem\', with \'Product\', \'ProductionOrder\', \'StockMovement\', and \'BudgetItem\' still being crafted. I\'ll include fields like \'notaFiscal\' and \'observacoes\'. I am considering the relationships between these entities.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will now create the `/src/types.ts` file. This file will hold all the TypeScript models and interfaces for our 3D printing management system, ensuring strong typing across inventory, sales, production, tariffs, and backups."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 9146528
    }
